<div class="panel section intro"><div class="kicker">ABOUT THIS PROJECT</div><h2>Hi, I'm Solo.</h2><p class="lead">I'm a speedcuber from Pakistan, and I built BLD CLUB to make learning 3×3 blindfolded solving clearer, more engaging and far more enjoyable.</p><p>3BLD has far fewer learning resources than the rest of cubing, and many of the ones that exist are hard to use: long spreadsheets of algorithms and commutators that are accurate, but dry and easy to abandon. This site takes a different approach and treats learning like a game. Work through the letter maps, drill random cases against a timer, track your solves, and step through every algorithm on an interactive 3D cube.</p><p>For now, BLD CLUB covers the setup I use myself: M2 for edges and Orozco for corners.</p><p>More is planned. I intend to add the beginner-friendly Old Pochmann method, along with Orozco edges, Eka, and 3-style for both edges and corners. I will publish each one once I have learned it properly and can teach it well, so that everything here stays accurate and well understood.</p><p class="sig">— Solo</p></div><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><b>24</b><small>OROZCO LETTERS</small></div><div class="stat"><b>24</b><small>M2 EDGE LETTERS</small></div><div class="stat"><b id="attempts">0</b><small>PRACTICE ATTEMPTS</small></div></div>
<div class="tiles"><div class="panel tile" onclick="page('m2')"><div class="emoji">🧩</div><h3>M2 edges</h3><p>My DF buffer, UB swap sticker, letter map, special cases and parity notes.</p></div><div class="panel tile" onclick="page('orozco')"><div class="emoji">🌀</div><h3>Orozco corners</h3><p>My UFR buffer, UBR helper, every first/second target algorithm and my commutator notation.</p></div><div class="panel tile" onclick="page('cube')"><div class="emoji">🎮</div><h3>3D cube lab</h3><p>Load an algorithm, move one step at a time, play it, pause it and reset the view.</p></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>MY SETUP</h2><div><b>Letter scheme:</b> Speffz<br><b>Edges:</b> M2 · DF buffer · UB swap sticker<br><b>Corners:</b> Orozco · UFR buffer · UBR helper</div></div>
</section>
<section id="foundations" class="view"><div class="eyebrow">LESSON 01 / FOUNDATIONS</div><h1>HOW <span>3BLD</span> WORKS</h1><p class="lead">A blindfolded solve has two big stages: memorise the targets, then execute the piece cycles. My letter scheme turns sticker locations into a sequence you can remember.</p>
<div class="tiles"><article class="panel"><span class="pill">01 · MEMO</span><h3>Targets and letters</h3><p class="muted">Each sticker has a letter. I trace where the piece in my buffer needs to go, creating a memo sequence.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="pill">02 · CYCLES</span><h3>Buffer and helper</h3><p class="muted">The buffer is the reference position for tracing. Orozco corners also use a helper position to handle cycles and target order.</p></article><article class="panel"><span class="pill">03 · EXECUTION</span><h3>Commutators</h3><p class="muted">A commutator combines move sequences so much of their effect cancels, leaving a controlled cycle.</p></article></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>MY COMMUTATOR NOTATION</h2><p class="lead">I define <code>[A,B] = A B A' B'</code>. For a setup commutator, I perform the setup, execute the commutator, then undo the setup.</p><div class="notice">Note: these are my algorithms and notation exactly as I use them. Nothing here has been auto-corrected or machine-verified.</div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>OROZCO TARGET ORDER</h2><p class="lead">My tip: the first target likes the helper; the second target likes the buffer. The first letter cycles buffer → target → helper. The second letter uses the reverse order: buffer → helper → target.</p></div>
</section>
<section id="m2" class="view"><div class="eyebrow">METHOD 01 / EDGES</div><h1><span>M2</span> EDGES</h1><p class="lead">M2 is my blindfolded edge method. My buffer is DF, my swapping sticker is UB, and the core swap is M2.</p><div class="tiles"><div class="panel"><span class="pill">BUFFER</span><h3>DF · U</h3><p class="muted">Letter U is the buffer and is skipped.</p></div><div class="panel"><span class="pill">SWAP STICKER</span><h3>UB · A</h3><p class="muted">Letter A is the UB swapping sticker.</p></div><div class="panel"><span class="pill">FORMULA</span><h3>Setup + M2</h3><p class="muted">Setup moves → M2 → undo setup moves.</p></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>M2 LETTER MAP & ALGORITHMS</h2><p class="muted">Use Load to send an algorithm straight to Cube Lab. Where a row lists alternatives separated by a slash, Load opens the first listed option.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="table" id="m2Table"><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Piece</th><th>Status</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Cube Lab</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>OPPOSITE-LETTER SPECIAL CASE</h2><p class="lead">If C, W, I or S appears as the second letter of a pair, use the algorithm of its opposite instead.</p><div class="row"><span class="pill">C ↔ W</span><span class="pill">I ↔ S</span></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>M2 PARITY OPTIONS</h2><div class="alg-grid" id="parityList"></div><div class="notice">I use options 1 and 3 with OP corners, and option 2 with Orozco corners.</div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>MY EXAMPLE MEMOS</h2><div class="alg"><b>Scramble 1</b><code>L F' B R F U D F2 D2 R' F2 D2 B2 R' U2 D2 L' U' L'</code><p class="muted">Edges memo: RS AC NV DJ BL GA. Since S is second in the pair, use I. Since C is second, use W.</p></div><div class="alg"><b>Scramble 2</b><code>U R B2 D2 L2 B2 L' F2 U2 L' F2 L' B U F R F' L' B L2</code><p class="muted">Edges memo: AL IX TV SB QD ER JR. I and S both appear first in their pairs, so use their own algorithms.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section id="orozco" class="view"><div class="eyebrow">METHOD 02 / CORNERS</div><h1><span>OROZCO</span> CORNERS</h1><p class="lead">My corner setup uses Speffz with UFR as the buffer and UBR as the helper. First-target cases use (1); second-target cases use (2).</p>
<div class="tiles"><div class="panel"><span class="pill">BUFFER</span><h3>UFR · C</h3><p class="muted">C is the buffer sticker. Skip it.</p></div><div class="panel"><span class="pill">HELPER</span><h3>UBR · B</h3><p class="muted">B is the helper sticker. Skip it.</p></div><div class="panel"><span class="pill">HELPER TWISTS</span><h3>N / Q</h3><p class="muted">In my letter map, N and Q are helper corner twists.</p></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>OROZCO LETTER MAP</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table class="table" id="cornerMap"><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Piece</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>ALL MY OROZCO ALGORITHMS</h2><p class="muted">The two cases are kept separate. Use the search box to find a letter or a move.</p><div class="searchbar"><input id="algSearch" class="input" placeholder="Search letter, move, or commutator…" oninput="renderOrozco()"><select id="caseFilter" class="input" style="max-width:190px" onchange="renderOrozco()"><option value="all">Both cases</option><option value="1">First target (1)</option><option value="2">Second target (2)</option></select></div><div class="alg-grid" id="orozcoAlgs"></div></div>
<div class="panel section"><h2>PARITY: MY EXECUTION NOTES</h2><p class="lead">Memorise corners first, then edges. Solve edges first, then corners. In my notes, an odd number of corner targets indicates parity.</p><div class="alg"><b>M2 edges → Orozco corners</b><code>D R2 D' M2 D R2 D'</code><p class="muted">The parity algorithm I use for M2 edges with Orozco corners.</p></div><div class="alg"><b>Ja perm option</b><code>x R2 F R F' R U2 r' U r U2</code><p class="muted">This is the Ja perm I use.</p></div><div class="alg"><b>Jb perm option</b><code>R U R' F' R U R' U' R' F R2 U R' U</code><p class="muted">My alternative: I memorise the edges so they finish with UF and UR swapped, then fix it with this Jb perm.</p></div></div>
</section>
